/*
 * Prestera 98DX3234 initialisation sequence for the Check Point L-72W.
 *
 * GENERATED -- do not edit by hand.
 *
 * Captured from the vendor firmware's own switch daemon by interposing
 * its /dev/mvPP ioctls, then replayed verbatim. Both the register writes
 * and the interleaved reads are kept, in the vendor's exact order.
 *
 * The replay discards every read result, so no read matters for its value.
 * They are kept anyway because they are not all alike: 3.7% are spins on a
 * completion bit (the indirect-table windows are poll-driven -- write data,
 * write index, write trigger, read until bit0 clears), and 73.6% are
 * read-modify-write reads left over from the vendor's SetRegField. Whether
 * the latter can be *removed*, as opposed to ignored, has not been measured;
 * they may still matter as bus transactions. See spec section 11.3.
 *
 * The sequence is idempotent. Measured by replaying it twice back to back on
 * one power-on: both passes completed with no transport errors, the SMI path
 * stayed healthy, and forwarding worked after each. An earlier note here
 * claimed the opposite -- that a second pass wedged MDIO and needed a power
 * cycle. That was wrong; the wedge it described came from an interrupted
 * sweep leaving the debug module's MDIO mutex held, which makes every read
 * return success without filling the caller's buffer.
 *
 * This is a blob, and it is documented as one. Reducing it to
 * comprehensible register programming is future work; see
 * docs/98dx3234-l72w-specs.md sections 9 and 10 for how much of it is
 * understood so far. Address bit31 carries the read flag, which is safe
 * because the largest address used here is 0x1d000200.
 */

